Hello E3B team,

My name is Rodrigo Castro, and I am seeking an academic collaborator for Listening.bio, a working environmental-audio platform that ingests WAV recordings, runs BirdNET, preserves raw model outputs, supports human review, and exports georeferenced biodiversity evidence.

The technical workflow has been verified with real public audio. The next step must be scientifically designed: a small New York City pilot with a repeatable sampling protocol, reference annotations, error analysis, and conservative interpretation.

I propose a study across three to five urban sites using 50 to 100 recordings. Listening.bio would provide the software, processing workflow, provenance controls, maps, and exports. I am looking for a faculty member, graduate researcher, or lab that could help shape the protocol, review a representative detection sample, and evaluate whether the resulting evidence is useful for urban ecology or conservation research.

The project is designed to treat AI predictions as reviewable candidates, not ecological truth. A successful pilot would produce a reusable protocol, an annotated validation sample, a transparent error report, and a foundation for a partner-led grant application.

Could you please route this inquiry to someone working in urban ecology, ornithology, conservation biology, quantitative ecology, or biodiversity monitoring? I would be glad to share a short demo and draft study protocol.

